			      playtaxman

				README

This folder contains the C++ source code for a command-line program
that plays the Taxman game using any of a variety of heuristic
strategies.

The rules of the Taxman game are as follows: initially the board
contains the numbers from 1 to some chosen N.  The player takes one of
the numbers, and the Taxman takes as tax all the numbers that evenly
divide it.  Those numbers are removed from the board, and the player
makes the next move.  The Taxman must get some tax on each move.  When
there are no numbers left that can legally be taken by the player, the
Taxman gets them all and the game ends.  The player's score is the sum
of the numbers taken by the player, and the Taxman's score is the sum
of the numbers taken by the Taxman.  The goal is to beat the Taxman.

Optimal play, beating the Taxman by the largest amount possible, is
probably NP-hard, which means it is not readily determined.
Therefore, various heuristic strategies have been devised that attempt
to do nearly as well as the optimal strategy.

To build this program, you need a C++ compiler.

If you have a Unix-like environment such as Linux or MacOS, you can
build the program from the command line with the command "make".

Otherwise, it should suffice simply to compile all of the *.cpp files
into one program.  You should specify at least some optimization
to ensure efficient operation.

This folder includes a bash script "runheuristics" to run the program
with game size N=21 for each of the heuristics.  The correct output is
in the file "runheuristics.out".  You can use this as a simple test
that the program works correctly.

Invoke the program with the command-line option "-?" to see a help
page listing all the options, including the supported heuristics.

Use the "-d" option to read a description of a given heuristic.

